    /// event's offsets only then (ADR-0021's fifth entry); the cell is resolved here.
    /// Its colour is <c>--ex-row-hover-background</c> (ADR-0029); the band is the
    /// overlay's, so no row learns anything and none re-renders.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool HighlightHoverRow { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Paints a Row Stripe on every second row of the result (ADR-0038) — appearance only,
    /// off unless asked for. Counted by the row's position in the whole result, so a
    /// stripe stays with its row while scrolling, across a pager's pages and on a
    /// Placeholder; Row Kind and Cell State paint over it, and the overlays above it. The
    /// colour is <c>--ex-row-stripe-background</c>. An explicit value beats a Wrapper's
    /// cascaded <see cref="GridPresentationDefaults.StripeRows"/>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool StripeRows { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What a Wrapper hands down (ADR-0030): the glyph widths of the font its Theme
    /// sets, and the Density its density word maps onto. Read only where the
    /// corresponding parameter was not written — an explicit parameter still wins,
    /// per value (ADR-0028).
    /// </summary>
    [CascadingParameter] public GridPresentationDefaults? Presentation { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised whenever the selection changes, including when it is dropped. The
    /// selected-cell count a status line shows is <see cref="GridSelection.CellCount"/>,
    /// which is the sum of the rectangles' areas and therefore needs no data at all
    /// (ADR-0014).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GridSelection> SelectionChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised when the rows on screen are not all in the Window: "I need these rows".
    /// The grid does not wait and does not fetch — it keeps painting Placeholders until
    /// a Window that covers them arrives (ADR-0001). Read-ahead, throttling and
    /// discarding stale answers belong to the Consumer.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<RowRange> OnRangeNeeded { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether an answer is in flight. Forgetting to pass it means a stale
    /// Window is shown as if it were current (ADR-0001).</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool IsLoading { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Sort in force, outermost first — what the header indicator and
    /// <c>aria-sort</c> are painted from, and what the next header click cycles from
    /// (ADR-0012/0023). The grid never sorts: the Consumer applies this and pushes the
    /// reordered Window in, bumping <see cref="RowSequenceVersion"/> (ADR-0001/0011).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<SortSpec>? Sorts { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised when a header click changes the sort (ADR-0012): the whole next Sorts
    /// list, not a delta. The grid changes nothing on its own — the painted order moves
    /// only when the Consumer answers with a new Window (ADR-0001).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<IReadOnlyList<SortSpec>> OnSortChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Cell Metadata, asked for by (row, column) and answered as one of the five states
    /// the grid understands (ADR-0006). The grid does not interpret the answer beyond
    /// painting a class for it — the Consumer's own vocabulary is mapped onto these five
    /// on the way in, and any accompanying data stays with the Consumer.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Hold it in a field and hand over a new one when the metadata changes.</b>
    /// The delegate's identity is the change signal, the way a row instance's is
    /// (ADR-0003): rewriting the dictionary behind a lookup that stays the same leaves
    /// the states already on screen where they are.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public CellStateOf<TRow>? CellState { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A per-cell kind (ADR-0050, item 6): asked for by (row, column), it overrides the
    /// column's <see cref="GridColumn{TRow}.Type"/> for that one cell's alignment, numeric
    /// class and <c>####</c> decision (ADR-0016) — a text heading above a column of
    /// amounts is left-aligned and never hashed, a number in a Text column is
    /// right-aligned and hashed when it does not fit. Sorting and filtering stay per
    /// column. Null — the default — leaves every cell on its column's kind, exactly as
    /// before. Asked of value cells only: an Action, Template or Mark cell paints no value
    /// to align or hash.
    ///
    /// <para>The text a cell paints is unchanged by it: the column's <c>Format</c> where it
    /// declares one, the value's own <c>ToString()</c> where it does not — the core's
    /// default format is the same for every kind, so there is nothing kind-dependent to
    /// follow.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Hold it in a field and hand over a new one when the kinds change.</b> Its
    /// identity is the change signal, as <see cref="CellState"/>'s is (ADR-0003/0006).</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TRow, GridColumn<TRow>, ColumnType>? CellType { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A per-cell alignment (ADR-0050, item 7): asked for by (row, column), it aligns that
    /// one cell. The precedence is this answer where it is not
    /// <see cref="Columns.CellAlign.Auto"/>, then the column's
    /// <see cref="GridColumn{TRow}.Align"/>, then the default of the cell's kind
    /// (<see cref="CellType"/>, then the column's; ADR-0016). A Sheet needs it for a user's
    /// own alignment and for Excel's centring of booleans and Error Values. Null, the
    /// default, leaves every cell aligned exactly as before. Asked of value cells only, as
    /// <see cref="CellType"/> is.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Hold it in a field and hand over a new one when the alignments change.</b>
    /// Its identity is the change signal, as <see cref="CellState"/>'s is
    /// (ADR-0003/0006).</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TRow, GridColumn<TRow>, CellAlign>? CellAlign { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A per-cell painted text (ADR-0050, item 11): asked for by (row, column) with the
    /// column's resolved content width and the <see cref="CellMetrics"/>, it answers the
    /// text the cell paints, or null for the value's own text. ExSheet paints General
    /// fitted to its column this way (ADR-0047). The <c>####</c> rule still decides on what
    /// is painted for a Number or Date cell (ADR-0016), and where the painted text differs
    /// from the value's own, the value's text is the cell's accessible name
    /// (ADR-0016/0033). Copy, editing, the value list and Auto widths read the value's own
    /// text, unchanged. Null, the default, paints every cell exactly as before. Asked of
    /// value cells only, as <see cref="CellType"/> is.
    ///
    /// <para><b>Hold it in a field and hand over a new one when the texts change.</b> Its
    /// identity is the change signal, as <see cref="CellState"/>'s is (ADR-0003/0006); a
    /// change of column width repaints the rows on its own.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public PaintedTextOf<TRow>? PaintedText { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What role each row plays — detail, group or total (ADR-0024). Null means every
    /// row is a detail row. Asked once per painted row, so it is as cheap as
    /// <see cref="CellState"/> has to be, and its identity is the change signal in the
    /// same way: hold it in a field and hand over a new one when the roles change.
    /// Changing what an unchanged delegate would answer does not repaint.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TRow, RowKind>? RowKind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised when an action in an Action Column is pressed: which row, which column,
    /// which action (ADR-0020). The grid does not perform it and holds no state about
    /// it — what a detail action opens happens outside the grid, and expanding inside a
    /// row is not possible anyway (ADR-0013).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GridActionEventArgs<TRow>> OnAction { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Row Marks on the push form (ADR-0043): who holds them, answers whether a row
    /// is marked, counts them, and takes each marking gesture. Needed only when a Mark
    /// Column is declared; a Source keeps its own, and passing both is refused — one of
    /// the two would have to silently win.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IRowMarks<TRow>? Marks { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The copy cap, in cells (ADR-0005). Past it the grid does not copy at all — it
    /// never copies the first N — and the refusal is strictly past: a selection of
    /// exactly the cap still copies. The Consumer can set it higher; the default is a
    /// safety valve, not a daily path.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public long CopyCellCap { get; set; } = ClipboardRules.DefaultCellCap;

    /// <summary>The default <see cref="PasteByteCap"/>: 16 MB (ADR-0005).</summary>
    public const long DefaultPasteByteCap = 16L * 1024 * 1024;

    /// <summary>
    /// The paste ceiling, in UTF-8 bytes across both flavours (ADR-0005). A paste crosses
    /// to .NET as streams, and a stream needs a ceiling; past it the paste is refused
    /// whole, before any of it is read — never pasted from the <c>text/plain</c> flavour
    /// alone, which is the display format and already rounded. On Blazor Server it is
    /// also what keeps one client from filling the server's memory.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public long PasteByteCap { get; set; } = DefaultPasteByteCap;

    /// <summary>
    /// The one Edit Intent a paste raises (ADR-0007/0014): all cells in one
    /// notification. The grid writes nothing — the screen changes when the Consumer
    /// returns new row instances. Rows off screen or not yet fetched are included in
    /// the target; that is intended (ADR-0014). A Consumer that will not write it calls
    /// <see cref="GridPasteIntent.Refuse"/> before the handler completes, and the Selection
    /// stays where it was (ADR-0050, item 3). One value of plain text alone on the clipboard —
    /// no table in <c>text/html</c> — over a Selection of more than one cell targets the
    /// top-left cell of the range made last only, and once accepted the Selection collapses to
    /// that cell (ADR-0014, amended 2026-09-29); one value copied as a table fills the Selection.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GridPasteIntent> OnPaste { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 3): a paste may spill. A block of several
    /// cells pasted onto one cell then writes the block with that cell at its top-left — one
    /// <see cref="OnPaste"/> intent whose plan is the block — and the block becomes the
    /// Selection, with the Focus at its top-left and nothing scrolled, so the count on display is
    /// the count written — unless the Consumer calls <see cref="GridPasteIntent.Refuse"/>,
    /// and then the Selection stays where it was. <c>Editable</c> is judged on the block before anything is raised,
    /// and a block running past the grid's last row or column is refused as
    /// <see cref="PasteRefusalReason.SpillPastExtent"/>. Declared by a Consumer that holds
    /// every cell, such as a Sheet. Off — the default — ADR-0014's refusal stands.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool PasteMaySpill { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 5): fill is enabled. The fill handle is painted
    /// at the bottom-right corner of the Selection's last range, in the selection overlay —
    /// none for a disjoint Selection, and none while an edit is open. Dragging it extends
    /// along one axis, the one the pointer went further along, with the target outlined; on
    /// release the grid raises one <see cref="OnFill"/> intent and writes nothing. A target
    /// covering a column that is not Editable is refused whole first, through
    /// <see cref="OnPasteRefused"/> — paste and fill go through one gate (ADR-0035). Off —
    /// the default — no handle is painted.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowFillHandle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Fill Intent (ADR-0050, item 5): a fill-handle drag, released — the source range,
    /// the target range beside it and the direction. The grid writes nothing; what a fill
    /// means is the Consumer's, which pushes new rows back as for any edit (ADR-0007). A
    /// release back inside the source, or after the order moved under the drag, raises
    /// nothing.
    ///
    /// <para>When the handler completes, the Selection becomes the source and the target
    /// together, as in Excel — unless the handler called
    /// <see cref="GridFillIntent.Refuse"/>, and then it stays on the source (ADR-0050, item 5
    /// refined). With no handler, nobody has accepted, and the Selection stays too.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GridFillIntent> OnFill { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Filter in force (ADR-0002/0009), on the push form: what the panel paints
    /// its current state from. The grid never filters — the Consumer applies this and
    /// pushes the narrowed Window in, bumping <see cref="RowSequenceVersion"/> when
    /// the sequence moved (ADR-0011).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public GridFilter? Filters { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when the filter panel applies or clears (ADR-0009): the whole
    /// next Filter, columns combined with AND. The painted rows move only when the
    /// Consumer answers with a new Window.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GridFilter?> OnFilterChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Answers the filter panel's value list on the push form (ADR-0009): the distinct
    /// values of one column under all applied filters except that column's own — the
    /// exclusion is the Consumer's to honour, since the Consumer owns the filter
    /// state. Pull, deliberately: transient UI data. Left null, every value list
    /// answers TooMany and the panel degrades to condition mode. A Source answers this
    /// itself.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<string, CancellationToken, Task<DistinctValues>>? OnDistinctValuesNeeded { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The substitutable UI seams (ADR-0009/0010). Null renders the built-in
    /// Chrome; a substitute changes rendering and nothing about behaviour — the
    /// commands, the operators and what a filter means stay the core's.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IGridChrome? Chrome { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The column menu's "hide this column" (ADR-0010): visibility is View
    /// State, so the grid notifies and the Consumer pushes back a Columns list without
    /// the column. The command renders only while this has a delegate.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> OnColumnHidden { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The column menu's "pin up to this column" (ADR-0010/0011): pinning
    /// changes through its own explicit act, never through a drag — and it notifies
    /// separately, one gesture, one piece of View State.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<int> OnPinnedCountChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Edit Intent (ADR-0007): a committed edit leaves the grid as row identity,
    /// column and new value, and the grid holds no committed value — the painted cell
    /// changes when the Consumer returns a new row instance. Uncommitted text is the
    /// grid's and never reaches this.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GridEditIntent<TRow>> OnEdit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Ctrl+Z, forwarded (ADR-0007): the Consumer owns the history, because only it sees
    /// every change — an edit in the grid and a row added through a dialog alike. The grid
    /// knows nothing to put in the notification and changes nothing on it. The key is taken
    /// from the page only while this has a listener, and never while the Cell Editor is
    /// open, where Ctrl+Z undoes the typing. ExSheet's undo stack is one such listener
    /// (ADR-0050, item 8, which points to ADR-0007 for this definition).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnUndo { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Ctrl+Y and Ctrl+Shift+Z, forwarded like <see cref="OnUndo"/> (ADR-0007) —
    /// both Excel spellings, on every platform.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnRedo { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Delete's Clear Intent (ADR-0054): the selected positions should hold no value. One
    /// notification for the whole selection, carrying no value — not a paste of empty text,
    /// which an amount column would read as a zero or a parse failure. Judged by the paste
    /// gate first: a selection covering a non-editable column is refused whole, through
    /// <see cref="OnPasteRefused"/>, and raises nothing here.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<GridClearIntent> OnClear { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A push-mode Consumer's answer to a Find step (ADR-0055): the next cell whose displayed
    /// text matches, in the order the request was read in. A bound <see cref="Source"/>
    /// answers through its own <c>FindAsync</c>, and passing this beside one is refused by
    /// name; with neither, Ctrl+F is refused rather than left to the browser, whose find sees
    /// only the painted rows. A step asked while one is out cancels it, and its answer is
    /// discarded. An answer outside the request — a column it did not name, a row the result
    /// does not have — throws, naming the request.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<GridFindRequest, CancellationToken, Task<GridFindResult>>? OnFind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A Find step that moved nothing, saying why (ADR-0055): nothing can search,
    /// nothing matched, or the order moved while the step was out. Chrome words it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<FindRefusalReason> OnFindRefused { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A refused copy, saying which rule it hit (ADR-0005): the clipboard was
    /// left untouched, and this is where Chrome learns why.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<CopyRefusalReason> OnCopyRefused { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A refused paste — or a refused Ctrl+Enter fill, which goes through the
    /// same rules, or a refused fill-handle drag (ADR-0050, item 5), whose one refusal is
    /// the editability reason — saying which rule it hit (ADR-0014 / 0035). The single-cell reason is
    /// the one whose message says reselecting a target of the same shape will work; the
    /// editability reason is the one where no reselection of that shape ever will.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<PasteRefusalReason> OnPasteRefused { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Text the user had typed and not committed, thrown away because the grid
    /// could no longer place it (ADR-0011): the order changed under the editor, or the
    /// row left the Window. Neither is the user's own doing — a Consumer-pushed sort or
    /// refresh lands and the typing goes — so the grid says so rather than leaving the
    /// loss to be guessed at. Chrome writes the sentence and announces it (A11Y-16); the
    /// grid holds no strings.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<EditDiscardReason> OnEditDiscarded { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The wording for a command id, for the menus the built-in Chrome renders
    /// (ADR-0036). The core names commands and does not name them in a language: this is
    /// how a Consumer keeping the built-in menu translates it, or renames one item,
    /// without reimplementing the menu. Returning null for an id falls back to
    /// <see cref="BuiltInCommandLabels"/>. A substituted Chrome never consults this — it
    /// has the ids and resolves its own wording. The built-in Formula Bar's two fields take
    /// their accessible names through it too, by <see cref="GridLabelIds"/> (ADR-0036/0051).</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<string, string?>? CommandLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The built-in Chrome's word for an id: the Consumer's through
    /// <see cref="CommandLabel"/>, the built-in English where it says nothing (ADR-0036).</summary>
    private string WordFor(string id) => CommandLabel?.Invoke(id) ?? BuiltInCommandLabels.For(id);

    /// <summary>The Consumer's own context-menu commands, appended to the core's
    /// (ADR-0010/0036). It is handed the target — the clicked row, the column, and the
    /// selection as rectangles plus the version they are written in — and returns
    /// commands whose <c>Invoke</c> closes over whatever it needs. Resolving an index
    /// into a row beyond the Window is the Consumer's own question; the grid does not
    /// hold those rows.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<ContextMenuContext<TRow>, IEnumerable<GridCommand>>? ContextCommands { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A cell's message, asked for by (row, column) — Cell Metadata's idiom
    /// verbatim (ADR-0034). It is <b>asked on demand</b>, at the moment a popover opens
    /// and never while painting: <c>CellStateOf</c> is consulted every render for every
    /// cell and stays a cheap enum, while the message is read for at most one cell at a
    /// time. Returning null means there is nothing to show and nothing opens.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TRow, GridColumn<TRow>, string?>? CellMessageOf { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Answers a copy whose selection runs beyond the Window on the push form
    /// (ADR-0005): asked for the rows of one range, under the order on screen. The
    /// answer is handed to the clipboard and stored nowhere. Left null, such a copy is
    /// refused as <see cref="CopyRefusalReason.RowsUnavailable"/> rather than emitting
    /// the fraction in hand. A Source answers this itself.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<RowRange, CancellationToken, Task<IReadOnlyList<TRow>>>? OnCopyRowsNeeded { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 9): the Consumer answers a copy. On both copy
    /// routes — the <c>copy</c> event's synchronous question and the asynchronous write a
    /// menu copy or a selection beyond the Window takes — the grid first applies its own
    /// rules (empty, misaligned, past the cap: ADR-0005), then asks this with the ranges
    /// being copied, and writes exactly the two flavours it returns. Asked synchronously and
    /// in process, so no rows are gathered for it and <see cref="OnCopyRowsNeeded"/> is not
    /// consulted. A refusal leaves the clipboard untouched, announces the Consumer's sentence
    /// through the root's live region, and raises <see cref="OnCopyRefused"/> as
    /// <see cref="CopyRefusalReason.RefusedByConsumer"/>. Left null — the default — the grid
    /// builds the copy itself, exactly as ADR-0005 says.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<GridCopyRequest, GridCopyAnswer>? CopyAnswer { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 1): a plain click on a column header selects
    /// the whole column, and nothing sorts — Excel's meaning, where ADR-0012 gives the click
    /// to sorting. Shift+click extends from the Focus's column, as it always does. Off, the
    /// header click sorts exactly as before.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool HeaderClickSelects { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 1): the Row Headings, a pinned band beside the
    /// rows labelled by this function — handed a row's absolute position in the result,
    /// Placeholders included, and answering its label (the row number, on a Sheet). The
    /// band stands outside the column index space: it is never in the Selection, a copy,
    /// Ctrl+A or the Enter/Tab cycle. A click on a heading selects the whole row and
    /// Shift+click extends; where the column header stands too, the corner where the two
    /// meet selects all. Null — the default — is no band, and hides the Row Headings.
    /// Asked once per painted row per render, so it has to be as cheap as
    /// <see cref="RowKind"/>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<int, string>? RowHeadings { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Row Headings' width, in pixels (ADR-0050/0028): resolved geometry, emitted
    /// inline, never a stylesheet value. Null estimates it from the Cell Metrics, as the
    /// wider of the first and the last row's labels — which is the widest for row numbers.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public double? RowHeadingWidth { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 1): hides the column header, taking the whole
    /// header band away — the first row starts at the Viewport's top. Also what a headerless
    /// list asks for. Whole columns stay reachable with Ctrl+Space.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool HideHeader { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 12): the column headers carry no column-menu
    /// button, and Alt+↓ opens no column menu, while the resize grips stay wherever
    /// <see cref="OnColumnWidthChanged"/> has a delegate. A double-click on a column's edge
    /// still sizes it to fit (ADR-0016), and a header's required width no longer reserves
    /// the button's band. A Sheet's Headings are Excel's, which carry no menu. Off — the
    /// default — the button is painted exactly as before, wherever some command could reach
    /// a Consumer.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool HideColumnMenu { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 13): the grid does not offer copy with headers.
    /// The Context Menu holds <see cref="GridCommandIds.Copy"/> without
    /// <see cref="GridCommandIds.CopyWithHeaders"/> — the context handed to
    /// <see cref="ContextCommands"/> included — and a copy asked with headers by any other
    /// route is refused, the clipboard left as it was, rather than written without them
    /// (ADR-0005). A Sheet's column letters are addresses, not headers (ADR-0048). Off — the
    /// default — the command is offered exactly as before.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool HideCopyWithHeaders { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0050, item 2): where Ctrl+arrow stops. Handed the Focus
    /// and the direction, it answers the cell on that line where the data ends — the grid
    /// does not hold the data, so it cannot know. Asked synchronously, on Ctrl+arrow and
    /// Ctrl+Shift+arrow only (Home and End keep their meaning); the Focus moves to the
    /// answer, or the range extends to it. An answer off the Focus's line, behind it or
    /// outside the grid is refused by name. Null — the default — goes to the grid's edge,
    /// as ADR-0012 has it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<CellPosition, GridDirection, CellPosition>? DataEdge { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0051): the text the Cell Editor opens on, per cell — on a
    /// Sheet, the Entry, so F2 on a Formula's cell shows <c>=A1*2</c> rather than its Value.
    /// Asked by (row, column) when an edit opens on the cell's text — F2, a double click, a
    /// click in the Formula Bar — and for the Formula Bar's display. Overwrite keeps its
    /// meaning: the typed character replaces. Null, or a null answer, opens on the value as
    /// before.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TRow, GridColumn<TRow>, string?>? EditorTextOf { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A Consumer declaration (ADR-0051): the Formula Bar, a band inside the root above the
    /// header holding the Name Box and the Focus cell's text — the opening text where
    /// <see cref="EditorTextOf"/> supplies one, the full value behind a <c>####</c> where
    /// not (ADR-0016). Its height is the Grid Metrics' (<see cref="GridMetrics.FormulaBarHeightPx"/>),
    /// and it comes out of <see cref="ViewportHeight"/>: the rows take what it leaves. Off —
    /// the default — there is no band and the geometry is as before.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowFormulaBar { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Name Box's label for the Focus, in the Consumer's words (ADR-0051): <c>D200</c> on
    /// a Sheet. Asked for the Focus cell whenever the Formula Bar paints — and while a pointing
    /// outline stands (ADR-0051's Point), for the outline's Focus, as Excel names the pointed
    /// cell; null, or a null answer, leaves the Name Box empty — the grid names no cell in
    /// words of its own.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<CellPosition, string?>? NameBoxLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Name Box's label for the size of a range while a drag selects it, in the Consumer's
    /// words (ADR-0052): <c>4R x 3C</c> on a Sheet, as Excel shows while the button is down.
    /// Asked for the range holding the Focus on each render of a selecting drag once it covers
    /// more than one cell; when the button comes up the Name Box names the Focus again through
    /// <see cref="NameBoxLabel"/>. A fill drag and a pointing drag are not asked. Null, or a
    /// null answer, keeps the Focus's label throughout. Keyboard extension is not asked: telling
    /// when Shift is released would need a <c>keyup</c> listener (ADR-0021).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<SelectionRange, string?>? NameBoxSizeLabel { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Enter in the Name Box (ADR-0051): the text the user typed there, handed to the
    /// Consumer, who resolves it to a position — the grid reads no address — and places the
